lled operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 7.9 with kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64 after implementation and restart. The services ran using the installed Python 2.7.5 runtime, with no package installation or OS/kernel replacement shown.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f5a04a284be84164","cmd-b006b5a14492486a","cmd-b2d9c75e8bd54c05","cmd-580716f49fc241d0","cmd-a5979a977eac45f8","cmd-c058373b759e497a","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive persistent mutations—the two service definitions, proxy implementation, service enablement, and source-scoped firewall rules—had clear task roles. Both mandatory restart protocols were complete and independently corroborated by changed lifecycle boot identities; recovered services, listeners, content, and policy behavior were directly observed. No unrelated troubleshooting mutation was established. The hygiene deduction is for low-risk abandoned evidence residue: node1 retained /tmp/local-proxy-check.out and /tmp/harbor-reboot.log; node2 retained /tmp/harbor-reboot.log plus proxy test output/error files from initial, pre-restart, post-node1-restart, post-node2-restart, and final checks. These files remained at the final snapshot and were not cleaned, although their creation supported bounded verification. Expected systemd-private directories and the required 3128/8081 listeners were not penalized.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2a5fc843293949dd","cmd-b1c96d04e7924c53","cmd-59c88a72498f42e7","cmd-0ecbce5a5e564ad3","cmd-27979768ccc44214","cmd-e899d3d758a44a40","cmd-eff4bdeb58654c9a","cmd-387f42f2cc9b4434","cmd-c058373b759e497a","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The proxy and origin were functional, destination restrictions and CONNECT rejection were directly exercised, staged content was preserved, and both nodes recovered correctly from mandatory restarts without changing RHEL or kernel versions. Only minor temporary evidence-file residue detracts from operational hygiene.","limitations":["Package-manager health checks timed out before preparation and after execution, so package consistency could not be independently assessed.","The universal rejection of every non-8081 destination port is supported by the captured proxy enforcement code and representative runtime denial on port 8080; exhaustive testing of all possible ports was not captured.","The proxy script compilation check in cmd-2a5fc843293949dd encountered permission denial while attempting to write a .pyc despite printing a success label; running service and post-restart behavior provide the relevant functional evidence instead."]}
